Wittgenstein’s thoughts on mathematical logic went through two revolutionary phases:
• Early Phase (Logical Determinism): Heavily influenced by Bertrand Russell and Gottlob Frege, he attempted to construct the world using perfect logical symbols. He believed that whatever can be said at all can be said clearly, and "whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent." Calculus and mathematical logic are the pinnacle of symbolic tools used to precisely define the world.
